LEAD = {
    "headline": "UNILEVER SAYS IT NOW HAS 500,000 PEOPLE MAKING CONTENT FOR IT, UP FROM 20,000 TWO YEARS AGO, AND ITS CHIEF EXECUTIVE TOLD INVESTORS THAT AFTER FOUR DAYS A VIDEO DOES NOT JUST STOP WORKING, IT STARTS LOSING MONEY",
    "deck": "One of the largest advertisers on earth has put a shelf life on video, out loud, in a published transcript.  Four days.  After that Fernando Fernandez says the return is not smaller, it is negative.  Read that number against how long your own videos take to make and something uncomfortable falls out.",
    "stamps": [
        ("UNILEVER INVESTOR EVENT  4 NOV", "https://www.unilever.com/files/unilever-investor-event-2026-transcript.pdf"),
        ("MARKETING DIVE  12 NOV", "https://www.marketingdive.com/news/how-unilevers-social-first-marketing-is-evolving-amid-reorganization/874457/"),
        ("AD WORLD ON WARC  10 NOV", "https://www.adworld.ie/2026/11/10/marketer-optimism-falls-for-a-third-year-as-warc-outlines-five-trends-for-2027/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Unilever held an investor event in New York on <mark>Wednesday 4 November</mark> and published the transcript.  Marketing Dive wrote it up yesterday, which is how most of the industry found out.  Chief executive Fernando Fernandez gave investors three numbers about content that are worth more than most of the research published this year.  First: \"we have now 500,000 content creators working for Unilever globally.\"  Second, on where that started: \"It was around 20,000 two years ago.\"  Third, on output: \"We have increased our content development in the last two years more than 12 times.\"",
        "Then the sentence that should stop you.  \"The lifespan of a video today is 4 days.\"  And immediately after it, in his own words: \"after 4 days, you don't have diminishing levels of return.  You have negative levels of return, negative marginal return.\"  Not fading.  Not tailing off.  Costing you money.  He names where Unilever's money goes while he says it: Meta, Google, TikTok, Amazon and Walmart.",
        "Now the mechanism, which is the part that changes a decision rather than just impressing a room.  Fernandez told investors that what drives Unilever's return is how well its content performs on the platform, and he put it plainly enough to repeat in a meeting: \"the level of engagement that you provide to the platform fundamentally changes the price you pay.\"  Marketing Dive's account of the same event takes it one step further.  Buying advertising through the five companies above no longer rewards whoever buys the most.  It rewards whoever earns the most engagement.  Unilever used to win by buying more advertising than almost anybody else.  It cannot win that way any more, so it is trying to win by making more content than anybody else, because the content is what sets the price.",
        "He is not the only one saying it, and that matters, because one chief executive with a reorganisation to sell is easy to discount.  WARC published its Marketer's Toolkit 2027 this week, built on a survey of <mark>1,000 marketing executives</mark> fielded in September and October.  Inside it, Marc Pritchard, chief brand officer at Procter and Gamble, gives the same instruction in a different accent: \"You need to make sure that you create memory in the algorithm.  What we've learned is that you need a much higher volume of content, a much higher variety of content, at a much higher velocity.\"  Two of the biggest advertisers on earth have independently landed on volume, variety and speed.",
        "What Unilever says this bought.  Return on investment \"increasing consistently, close to 10% to 15% a year\", and a dramatic fall in the cost of producing each piece of content.  Marketing Dive puts firmer figures on both halves: <mark>twelve times the content of 2024 at 90 percent lower cost</mark>, and creator-made content returning <mark>1.5 times what brand-led content returns</mark>.  It also reports that Unilever intends to move <mark>half of its digital advertising spend</mark> into social-first and creator-driven work.",
        "One honest thing to hold against the whole package.  Unilever will not say what a \"content creator working for Unilever\" actually is, so 500,000 counts an undefined relationship, and it will cover everything from a contracted partner to somebody whose clip was licensed once for pocket money.  Nor does Unilever publish any workings behind the four days, which is a single company-wide average sitting on top of wildly different categories and platforms.  What the number tells you reliably is what one of the largest buyers of advertising in the world now believes, and that belief is already changing what it commissions.",
    ],
    "numbers": [("4 DAYS", "stated video lifespan, per Unilever"), ("500,000", "creators working for Unilever"), ("12x", "content growth in two years")],
    "flagnote": "Every Unilever figure here is Unilever's own, given to investors, and none of it has been independently measured.  We have separated two tiers of sourcing.  The four-day lifespan, the negative-return wording, the 500,000 creators, the 20,000 two years ago, the more-than-twelve-times content growth and the 10 to 15 percent annual return improvement are all verbatim in Unilever's own published transcript and attributed to Fernandez.  The 90 percent lower cost per piece, the 1.5 times creator return and the plan to shift half of digital spend come from Marketing Dive's reporting of the same event; the part of the transcript we were able to read says only that the cost fell dramatically, with no figure, so treat those three as reported rather than confirmed.  Unilever does not define what a content creator working for Unilever is, does not say what any of them are paid, and publishes no workings behind the four days.  Fernandez was presenting a 2027 reorganisation at the same event, so he had every reason to make the case sound settled.  The WARC figures are from a sentiment survey and reach us through Ad World's write-up, because WARC's own page sits behind a subscription.",
    "so_what": "If an asset genuinely stops earning after four days, the expensive problem in your business is not the film, it is the calendar.  A twelve-week production schedule for one hero video is buying something that expires in less time than the legal review took.  And the reason a company this size is behaving this way is not fashion, it is pricing: the platforms charge you less when your content earns attention, so volume is a way of lowering your media bill rather than a way of filling a feed.  That is a different argument from the one most people are having about creator content.",
    "do_this": "Take your last four campaigns and plot views, clicks and sales day by day for the first three weeks, then mark where each one actually flattened.  Put that chart on the first slide of your next planning meeting.  If the curve really dies inside a week, commission six smaller things on a rolling schedule rather than one big thing in eight weeks, and move the approval steps that eat the most calendar time to the brief stage where they cost nothing.",
}

